{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_017oa6EioNxpoh9RTr9sQAGF", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_vrtx_01Q1ww9hofQe4Z1s329UNyct", "name": "Edit", "input": {"replace_all": false, "file_path": "/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v3/cases/case-003-opinion-piece-pov-voice/source.md", "old_string": "The recent trend toward AI-generated code has garnered significant attention from industry observers and practitioners alike. Experts argue that these tools play a crucial role in enhancing developer productivity, while some critics argue that the quality of generated code could potentially be problematic.\n\nIt is important to note that the technology showcases both promise and peril. The landscape of AI-assisted development is evolving rapidly, and it could be argued that the implications are far-reaching. Additionally, the interplay between human creativity and machine efficiency highlights the intricate tapestry of modern software engineering.\n\nOrganizations that leverage these tools effectively will be well-positioned to navigate the evolving landscape. In order to achieve optimal outcomes, teams must foster a culture of responsible AI adoption, ensuring that the technology serves as a complement to human expertise rather than a replacement.\n\nThe future looks bright for AI-assisted development. Exciting times lie ahead as the industry continues its journey toward a new paradigm of collaborative intelligence.", "new_string": "AI coding assistants are everywhere now. GitHub reports over 1.8 million paying Copilot users. Every major IDE has at least one AI plugin. The question stopped being \"should we use these?\" sometime in 2024.\n\nThey do speed things up, sometimes. Boilerplate, test scaffolding, config files, the stuff nobody wanted to write by hand anyway. But I keep running into the same problem: the code looks right, compiles fine, and does the wrong thing. You stop reading carefully because the suggestion seems plausible, and that is exactly when it burns you.\n\nThe real issue is not whether AI can write code. It can, sort of. The issue is whether your team reviews AI-generated code with the same rigor as human-written code. Most don't. A 2024 Uplevel study found no measurable difference in pull-request throughput between teams using AI assistants and those without. Faster first drafts do not mean faster shipping if review and debugging eat the difference.\n\nIf you are adopting these tools, skip the cultural manifestos. Start with tests. If you do not have tests, you cannot tell whether the suggestion is correct. That is the whole story."}}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 441, "cache_read_input_tokens": 39011, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 441, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 1}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "1e8c7993-63e6-4196-8a8e-83a34e211ec3", "uuid": "9c2f45b7-ac8e-4f78-8b44-f95c11f463c6", "timestamp": "2026-05-04T11:35:40.926Z"}
